          </div>\n   617→            </div>\n   618→\n   619→            \n   620→            <section>\n   621→                <h2>📚 Book Structure</h2>\n   622→                \n   623→                <p style=\"margin-top: 1rem; line-height: 1.8;\">This book contains 9 chapters. Chapters have descriptive titles.</p>\n   624→                \n   625→                \n   626→            </section>\n   627→\n   628→            \n   629→            <section>\n   630→                <h2>📖 Plot Summary</h2>\n   631→                <div style=\"margin-top: 1rem; line-height: 1.8; white-space: pre-wrap;\">Mrs. Sigourney Howard, recounting her experiences from the summer of 1922, begins her story in West Egg, where she rents a modest home between the opulent estates of East and West Egg. Her cousin Daisy Buchanan and her husband Tom, a wealthy, aggressive man with racist views, draw her into their world of privilege and moral decay. During a dinner at Tom’s East Egg mansion, Daisy’s fragile demeanor and her cynical wish for her daughter to be a 'beautiful little fool' reveal the hollowness beneath the surface of their lives. The mysterious figure of Jay Gatsby, whose extravagant parties light up the West Egg nights, captures her attention—especially when she witnesses him reaching toward a distant green light across the bay, a symbol of his unattainable dream.\n   632→\n   633→As the summer unfolds, Mrs. Howard becomes entangled in the lives of those around her. She accompanies Tom to the desolate Valley of Ashes, where he introduces her to his mistress, Myrtle Wilson, whose tragic fate unfolds in a violent outburst when Tom breaks her nose after she taunts him with Daisy’s name. At one of Gatsby’s lavish parties, she meets Jordan Baker, a fellow golfer whose dishonesty mirrors the moral ambiguity of the era. Through Jordan, she learns of Gatsby’s past love for Daisy, and she helps orchestrate a reunion between the two, which culminates in a tearful, overwhelming moment when Gatsby shows Daisy his wealth and past. Yet even as Gatsby’s dream seems within reach, Mrs. Howard senses its fragility, especially when Tom exposes Gatsby’s criminal ties and Daisy, overwhelmed, confesses she once loved both men.\n   634→\n   635→The climax arrives on a sweltering day when Gatsby and Daisy’s affair is revealed during a tense luncheon and a drive to the Plaza Hotel, where Tom forces a confrontation. After Myrtle Wilson is killed in a hit-and-run by a yellow car—later confirmed to be Gatsby’s—Tom manipulates the situation to frame Gatsby, leading to Wilson’s descent into madness. Gatsby, believing Daisy will stand by him, takes the blame, only to be shot dead by Wilson in his own mansion. In the aftermath, Mrs. Howard is left to confront the emptiness of the East’s elite: Daisy and Tom vanish without a trace, Wolfshiem refuses to attend the funeral, and only Gatsby’s father and a few strangers attend his burial. Reflecting on the tragedy, she realizes the futility of Gatsby’s dream and the moral rot of the world she once admired. In her final moments, she returns to the beach, erasing a vulgar word from Gatsby’s steps and contemplating the enduring human struggle to reclaim the past, ultimately resolving to return home, disillusioned but wiser.</div>\n   636→                \n   637→                <p style=\"margin-top: 1rem; color: var(--muted);\">\n   638→                    <strong>Themes:</strong> identity, ambition, loss\n   639→                </p>\n   640→                \n   641→            </section>\n   642→            \n   643→\n   644→            \n   645→            <section>\n   646→                <h2>🤖 Analysis Models</h2>\n   647→                <table style=\"width: 100%; margin-top: 1rem; border-collapse: collapse;\">\n   648→                    <thead>\n   649→                        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 2px solid var(--primary);\">\n   650→                            <th style=\"text-align: left; padding: 0.75rem; colo