   790→            <h3 style=\"margin-top: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; color: var(--accent-soft);\">Chapters</h3>\n   791→            \n   792→            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n   793→                <h4>\n   794→                    <span>Chapter 1</span>\n   795→                    <span class=\"meta\">5,037 words &middot; 33m</span>\n   796→                </h4>\n   797→                \n   798→                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter opens as the narrator, Nick Carraway, arrives by car at a modest house in West Egg, where a stranger gives him directions, granting him a sense of belonging; he purchases twelve volumes on banking and investment as symbols of his new beginning. He describes the geography of East and West Egg—twin egg-shaped peninsulas separated by a bay—and notes his rented home, priced at $80/month, nestled between two opulent estates, one belonging to the enigmatic Gatsby. That evening, he drives by car to East Egg to dine with his cousin Daisy Buchanan and her husband Tom, whose aggressive demeanor and racist recitations from The Rise of the Coloured Empires dominate the conversation. During a candlelit dinner on the porch, Daisy accuses Tom of bruising her knuckle, and after a telephone call interrupts them, Jordan Baker reveals in hushed tones that Tom has a mistress in New York. Daisy later confides in Nick her cynical hope that her daughter will be a 'beautiful little fool,' a statement Nick perceives as a hollow performance. As Nick returns home to West Egg, he stops on his lawn and observes Gatsby standing alone, arms outstretched toward a distant green light across the bay, before Gatsby vanishes into the night.</div>\n   799→                \n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports