{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XVCLGcbAdmW66aJXJuEHSP","type":"tool_result","content":"                    <span class=\"tag\">Owl-eyed man</span>\n                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">In the aftermath of Gatsby’s death, the narrator is overwhelmed by the media frenzy at Gatsby’s mansion, where police, photographers, and curious onlookers swarm the property, while false testimonies from Catherine and the detective’s casual labeling of Wilson as a 'madman' simplify the tragedy into a sensationalized narrative. Desperate to connect with those who knew Gatsby, the narrator makes futile attempts to reach Daisy and Meyer Wolfshiem—only to learn Daisy and Tom have vanished without a trace, and Wolfshiem refuses to attend the funeral, citing fear of being 'mixed up' in the affair. On the third day, Gatsby’s father, Henry C. Gatz, arrives by train from Minnesota, emotionally shattered yet proud, showing a worn copy of Hopalong Cassidy with Gatsby’s childhood self-improvement schedule; only Gatz and a Lutheran minister attend the funeral in a drizzling rain, as Klipspringer calls to request his tennis shoes and no other guests appear. At the cemetery, the narrator recognizes the man with owl-eyed glasses, who silently joins the procession, and after the grave is covered in canvas, he murmurs 'Amen to that' in response to the somber remark 'Blessed are the dead that the rain falls on,' while quietly calling Gatsby 'the poor son-of-a-bitch.' The narrator, now disillusioned by the East’s moral decay, reflects on his Western roots during a train journey recalling Christmas in Chicago, realizes he and his circle—Tom, Daisy, Jordan, Gatsby—are all out of place in the East, and resolves to return home. He confronts Jordan Baker, who reveals her engagement to another and accuses him of dishonesty; he admits he is too old to lie to himself and they part unresolved. Later, he encounters Tom Buchanan on Fifth Avenue, who coldly admits he told Wilson Gatsby owned the car that killed Myrtle, justifying it as necessary, and leaves to buy a pearl necklace or cuff buttons, leaving Nick feeling as though he spoke to a child. On his final night, the narrator visits the overgrown mansion, erases a vulgar word scrawled on the steps with his shoe, and walks to the beach, where he contemplates Gatsby’s futile pursuit of the green light and the enduring human struggle against time, pulled back into the past.</div>\n                                <strong>Owl Eyes</strong>\n                            <td class=\"aliases\">Owl</td>\n                            <td><strong>owl-eyed</strong></td>\n                                \"......Of course, of course! They’re fine!” and he added hollowl...\"\n                            <td><strong>finger-bowls</strong></td>\n                            <td><strong>hollowly</strong></td>\n                            <td><strong>Owl-eyes</strong></td>\n                            <td><strong>finger-bowls</strong></td>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"60ef33c9-85f9-4180-93d9-edfdc048cd8d","uuid":"294f5ac3-927a-4dd7-8a92-7749340e73ee","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"                    <span class=\"tag\">Owl-eyed man</span>\n                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">In the aftermath of Gatsby’s death, the narrator is overwhelmed by the media frenzy at Gatsby’s mansion, where police, photographers, and curious onlookers swarm the property, while false testimonies from Catherine and the detective’s casual labeling of Wilson as a 'madman' simplify the tragedy into a sensationalized narrative. Desperate to connect with those who knew Gatsby, the narrator makes futile attempts to reach Daisy and Meyer Wolfshiem—only to learn Daisy and Tom have vanished without a trace, and Wolfshiem refuses to attend the funeral, citing fear of being 'mixed up' in the affair. On the third day, Gatsby’s father, Henry C. Gatz, arrives by train from Minnesota, emot